Detection of Parkinson’s Disease from Biomedical Voice Patterns
A comparative machine learning study using Gaussian Naive Bayes, Polynomial SVM, and Gradient Boosting to classify Parkinson’s disease from biomedical voice measurements [file:132]. This project uses the UCI Parkinson’s Disease dataset and evaluates how different supervised learning models perform on a real-world healthcare classification problem [file:132].
Overview
Parkinson’s disease is a progressive neurological disorder that affects motor control and speech production, making voice-based biomarkers a useful signal for early detection [file:132]. This project investigates whether machine learning models can distinguish between healthy individuals and Parkinson’s patients using acoustic voice features such as jitter, shimmer, harmonics-to-noise ratio, and pitch variation [file:132].
Objectives
•	Build a binary classification pipeline to predict Parkinson’s disease status from biomedical voice features [file:132].
•	Compare the performance of Gaussian Naive Bayes, Support Vector Machine with polynomial kernel, and Gradient Boosting Machine [file:132].
•	Apply preprocessing, stratified train-test splitting, and grid-based hyperparameter tuning to improve model quality [file:132].
•	Evaluate model performance using accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and confusion matrices [file:132].
Dataset
This project uses the UCI Parkinson’s Disease Classification Dataset, which contains 195 voice recordings from 31 subjects, with 22 biomedical voice measurements and a binary target label indicating Parkinson’s disease status [file:132]. The dataset includes features related to:
•	Jitter and pitch variation
•	Shimmer and amplitude variation
•	Harmonics-to-noise ratio
•	Non-linear voice dynamics
•	Fundamental frequency characteristics
Methods
Models Compared
•	Gaussian Naive Bayes (GNB) [file:132]
